 Typo pass: purchaseControlers, stockMovementControler, "recived", "This acount not activated", "Inventory systen".
 Move authorize middleware out of user_controllers.js into a dedicated middleware file.
 Strip the leftover course-era hpp whitelist fields (duration, ratingsQuantity, etc.).
 Add structured logging (pino/winston) + request-ID correlation — morgan('dev') is dev-only, so production has zero request tracing across audit log / error controller / server logs.
 Add tests. Priority order: purchase receive/cancel, stock adjustment math, RBAC matrix, refresh rotation — these are exactly the places silent regressions are expensive.
🔴 FRONTEND — Critical

None, honestly — the frontend's issues are mostly UX debt and dead code, not correctness bugs. That itself is a good sign about how it was built.

🟠 FRONTEND — High Priority
 Topbar search is decorative — styled input with no state, no handler, no navigation. Either wire it to a real global/products search or remove it so it doesn't look broken.
 isOutgoingMovement includes purchase_cancel as an outgoing arrow, but the backend never writes that movement type. Cosmetic today, but it's a landmine if backend cancel-reversal ever gets implemented (see cross-cutting) — the frontend logic needs to be revisited together with that backend change, not independently.
 No skeleton loaders on list pages — only InlineLoader/EmptyState. On a 30s staleTime + real network latency, pages will visibly "pop in" content, especially on first load.
 Every mutation invalidates the whole query instead of optimistic updates — fine at current scale, but on Products/Movements (potentially large lists) this means a full refetch after every single edit. Worth introducing useMutation helpers with optimistic cache updates for at least Products and Adjustments, the highest-frequency mutation pages.
🟡 FRONTEND — Medium
 DashboardStats type is dead and the dashboard's client-side computation (limit: 1000 products + limit: 300 movements, computed locally) is doing real aggregation work in the browser that the backend could do in one query. See cross-cutting section — this is the highest-leverage fix available right now, in either codebase.
 Two separate admin-detection mechanisms — role.name === "Admin" for routes vs. empty-permissions-array = superuser in hasPermission. Consolidate into one source of truth so a future permission-matrix change doesn't silently desync route-level and button-level gating.
 Resource/action strings duplicated between src/api/* and src/lib/permissions.ts and the backend's permission matrix — three places that must stay in sync by hand. A shared constants file (or codegen from the backend's seed data) would remove an entire class of "button doesn't show even though the role has permission" bugs.
 errorMessage() called individually at every call site — a single interceptor-level toast on unhandled/unexpected errors would cut a lot of repeated try/catch boilerplate, while still letting individual mutations override with a custom message when needed.
 Large page components (Products.tsx 400+ lines) — extract modal forms into src/components/forms/.
🟢 FRONTEND — Low / Cleanup
 branding.ts.logoUrl is empty while logoMark is set — either drop it or wire to /favicon.svg.
 No lint/prettier config, no tests (Vitest/RTL) — worth adding before the page components grow further.
 No CSV/export on list pages — common ask for inventory tools once real users show up (Purchases, Movements, Audits are the obvious candidates).
