 Tailwind 3.4 with no v4 migration plan — not urgent, just flag it so it doesn't become a surprise later.
🔵 CROSS-CUTTING (Full-Stack) — do these together

These are the highest-leverage items because they require touching both sides in the same change, and each one currently has the frontend compensating for something the backend should just do.

Wire up a real dashboard endpoint. The backend already has getInventoryDashboard (aggregation for totals/stock value/low-stock/out-of-stock counts) sitting unused with one bug (soft-delete filter). Right now the frontend fetches up to 1000 products and 300 movements on every dashboard load and computes stats in the browser — that's real payload weight and real client CPU for something one Mongo aggregation pipeline does instantly. Task: fix the soft-delete filter, add a route (GET /api/v1/products/dashboard or similar), mount it with a short cache TTL, then swap DashboardPage over to it and delete the dead DashboardStats type reference. This is probably the single best ROI item on this whole list.
search limitation is consistent today but fragile. Both sides currently agree (frontend only exposes search on Products, backend only supports it on Products) — but that agreement is implicit, not enforced. If backend search gets extended to Suppliers, for example, someone has to remember to also update the frontend, and vice versa. Worth a comment or shared type flagging which resources support search, so the two docs (and codebases) can't silently drift.
Purchase-cancel semantics need one shared decision. Backend docs say cancel doesn't reverse stock; frontend's isOutgoingMovement treats purchase_cancel as an outgoing movement that's never actually written. Decide once — implement full reversal, or formally document "cancel only applies to pending POs, no stock effect" — and update the schema enum, the frontend arrow logic, and the docs together so they can't drift further.
No transactionality is a frontend risk too. Once the backend adds mongoose.startSession() transactions to receive/adjust (recommended above), the frontend's optimistic-update strategy (if you build it, per the Medium item above) should be built with that in mind — a transaction means "succeeds or doesn't," which is exactly what makes optimistic UI safe. Sequence these together: do backend transactions first, then build frontend optimistic mutations on top.
Neither side has tests. If you're going to invest in tests at all, prioritize the workflows where frontend and backend both touch the same fragile logic: purchase receive → stock update → notification → dashboard number change. An end-to-end test of that one flow catches regressions in both codebases at once, which is more efficient than writing backend unit tests and frontend component tests separately at this stage.
Suggested order of attack

If I were sequencing this myself: (1) soft-delete unique index fix + (2) wire up the dashboard endpoint (kills two birds — fixes backend dead code and removes frontend's heaviest query pattern) → (3) transactions on receive/adjust → (4) resolve purchase-cancel semantics on both sides → (5) everything else in the Medium/Low buckets as time allows.

