Here's each issue stated plainly, with the most direct fix for each.

BACKEND

1. Soft-deleted records block recreation (unique index collision)
email, sku, referenceNo, and name fields are unique: true at the schema level, but soft-delete only filters reads. Delete a user/category/product, try to recreate it with the same value → E11000 error.
→ Fix: convert these to partial unique indexes: { email: 1 }, { unique: true, partialFilterExpression: { deletedAt: null } }. Mongo enforces it, no app logic needed. One migration, apply to all six unique fields.

2. Purchase receive / stock adjustment aren't transactional
receivePurchaseOrder updates items in parallel with Promise.all. If one item fails mid-loop, earlier items are already applied but the purchase stays pending. Retrying re-applies them.
→ Fix: wrap both in mongoose.startSession().withTransaction(). Needs a replica-set Mongo (Atlas has this already; local dev needs --replSet on a single node). This is the fix, not a mitigation — do it before anything else touches this code path.

3. Purchase cancel doesn't reverse stock
Cancelling a purchase that was already received leaves stock as-is. The purchase_cancel movement type exists in the schema but is never written. Right now cancel is only allowed on pending POs, so there's no live bug — but the schema/docs still imply reversal happens.
→ Fix: decide once — either build reversal for received→cancelled, or delete the purchase_cancel enum value and update docs to say "cancel only applies to pending orders." Don't leave it implying behavior that doesn't exist.

4. ?search= throws a 500 on every model except Product
Only Product has a Mongo text index. GET /categories?search=x throws a raw "text index required" error.
→ Fix: one-line guard in APIFeatures.filter() — if the model has no text index, drop the search param silently instead of passing it to Mongo.

5. my-* endpoints have no pagination
myPurchases/myAdjustments/myMovements/myAudits return everything, unlike getAll* which uses APIFeatures.
→ Fix: route them through the same APIFeatures pipeline you already have. Small, mechanical change, do it now before someone's history grows into the thousands.

6. generatePurchaseRef has a count-based race
Two POs in the same month can generate the same reference number; mitigated by 5 retries, not eliminated.
→ Fix: replace the count-and-guess with an atomic findOneAndUpdate on a dedicated Counter collection ({ month, seq }, $inc). Removes the race instead of retrying around it.

7. getInventoryDashboard exists, is unused, and has one bug
It's a correct aggregation (totals, stock value, low/out-of-stock) except it doesn't filter soft-deleted products. No route currently points to it.
→ Fix: fix the soft-delete filter, mount a route, cache it (60s is fine). This also fixes a frontend problem — see below.

8. Retroactive cost price overwrite
receivePurchaseOrder updates product.costPrice to the latest purchase cost, silently rewriting historical stockValue calculations for anything already in stock.
→ Fix: not urgent, but if you want accurate margins later, store unitCostAtReceipt on the movement record rather than mutating the live cost retroactively.

9. Swagger is configured but never mounted
→ Fix: app.use('/api-docs', swaggerUi.serve, swaggerUi.setup(swaggerSpec)). Five minutes, do it whenever convenient.

10. Dead code / cleanup
restrictTo, isLoggedIn, unused createUser, inconsistent response shapes (204 with/without body), profile returning 201, typos ("recived", "Inventory systen"), leftover course-era hpp whitelist.
→ Fix: batch these into one cleanup PR when you're already touching those files for something else — not worth a dedicated pass on their own.

FRONTEND

1. Topbar search box does nothing
Styled input, no state, no handler. Typing produces no result.
→ Fix: either wire it to a real search (route to /products?search=, or a global command palette if you want more), or remove it. A fake search box is worse than no search box.

2. Dashboard computes stats client-side by over-fetching
Fetches up to 1000 products + 300 movements on every dashboard load, computes totals/low-stock/stock-value in the browser. DashboardStats type exists but is unused.
→ Fix: this is solved by backend fix #7 above. Once the dashboard endpoint is mounted, swap DashboardPage to call it and delete the client-side computation. Do these two fixes as one PR spanning both repos.

3. isOutgoingMovement treats purchase_cancel as outgoing
The backend never writes that movement type, so this branch is currently unreachable dead logic — harmless today, but it'll silently misbehave if backend fix #3 (cancel reversal) ever ships without a matching frontend update.
→ Fix: no action needed right now, just note it as coupled to backend issue #3 — fix both in the same change whenever that happens.

4. No skeleton loaders on list pages
Only InlineLoader/EmptyState — content visibly pops in on load.
→ Fix: add a simple skeleton row component to the ui/ kit, use it in Table-based pages. Low effort, real UX improvement.

5. Every mutation invalidates the whole query
No optimistic updates anywhere — every create/edit/delete triggers a full refetch.
→ Fix: don't do this yet. Sequence it after backend transactions (fix #2) land — optimistic UI is only safe to build once the backend guarantees "succeeds fully or fails fully." Doing it before that just means the UI can show a state that never actually committed.

6. Two mechanisms for "is admin"
Routes check role.name === "Admin"; hasPermission treats an empty permissions array as superuser. Two different tests for the same idea.
→ Fix: pick one — probably keep the permission-based check since it's more granular — and make route guards use hasPermission too instead of a separate name check.

7. Resource/action strings duplicated three times
src/api/*, src/lib/permissions.ts, and the backend's permission matrix (seed data) all hardcode the same strings independently.
→ Fix: create one shared constants file on the frontend (resources.ts with PRODUCTS_CREATE = 'products:create' etc.) and reference it everywhere instead of raw strings. Doesn't fix the frontend/backend duplication, but stops the three-way drift on the frontend side, which is where it currently causes real bugs (buttons not showing).

8. errorMessage() called manually at every mutation site
→ Fix: add a default onError handler at the QueryClient/mutation-defaults level that toasts automatically; let individual mutations override only when they need a custom message.

9. Cleanup
branding.ts.logoUrl empty and unused, large page components (Products.tsx 400+ lines), no lint/prettier, no tests, no CSV export.
→ Fix: same as backend — batch into cleanup work, not worth blocking on. Tests are the one item here I'd prioritize if you have to pick one, since nothing currently guards against regressions on either side.

